  "notes": [
    {
      "title": "Twisting can involve retention as well as replacement",
      "text": "§27–213 covers deceptive or incomplete comparisons intended to induce forfeiture, surrender, retention, exchange, conversion or lapse. Do not classify every replacement as illegal or assume a retention pitch cannot be deceptive.",
      "example": "A producer who lies about a competing policy to prevent a client from leaving can still create a twisting issue.",
      "topics": [
        "1.2.11"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "The $50 exception has both a type and a condition",
      "text": "§27–209(a)(4) names educational materials, promotional materials and merchandise costing no more than $50. Subsection (b) prohibits conditioning receipt on a sale or purchase. The statute separately permits qualifying insurer products and services under stated conditions; these are not unrestricted producer cash rebates.",
      "example": "A qualifying notebook offered regardless of purchase and a premium refund contingent on purchase require different analysis.",
      "topics": [
        "1.2.10"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Specific life underwriting protections",
      "text": "Under §27–208(a), actuarial justification may be considered for a physical handicap or disability other than blindness or hearing impairment. The statute also addresses specified harmless genetic traits. Past lawful travel cannot alone support the listed adverse life-insurance actions. Specific future lawful travel can be treated differently only under the stated substantiated-risk conditions; a State Department advisory cannot be the sole supporting data source.",
      "example": "Do not use a blanket rule that all travel or all disability automatically justifies a higher life rate.",
      "topics": [
        "1.2.10"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Ordinary recordkeeping is not financial falsification",
      "text": "§27–205 addresses knowingly false statements of insurer financial condition and deceptive false entries or material omissions in insurer records. §27–204 addresses the specified injurious statements about financial condition.",
      "example": "An honest correction of an accounting error differs from hiding a liability to fool an examiner.",
      "topics": [
        "1.2.08"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "A referral label cannot authorize selling",
      "text": "Under §10–130, review what a recipient actually does and the applicable payment exception. This lesson does not create a blanket dollar limit for all referrals or permission to compensate unlicensed negotiation. Blank signed forms create a separate accuracy and signature-control problem; follow truthful application and insurer correction requirements.",
      "example": "The receptionist schedules Marcus’s meeting; the licensed producer explains coverage and makes the recommendation.",
      "topics": [
        "1.2.11",
        "1.2.04"
      ]
    }
  ],
